## 60. Unknown Information

Suppose:

> “A is north of B. C is east of A.”

The system can infer:

```text
C is north-east of B
```

but it cannot infer an exact distance unless distances are supplied.

This is an important rule:

> **Reason with known information, but never manufacture missing information.**

## 61. Spatial State

A navigation agent can maintain:

```text
SpatialState {
    position
    heading
    path_distance
    world_frame
    confidence
}
```

Example:

```text
position = (5,3)
heading = East
path_distance = 8 km
```

Each instruction updates the state.

## 62. State Transitions

For:

```text
Move(distance, direction)
```

the state transition is:

```text
new_position = old_position + displacement
```

For:

```text
Turn(angle)
```

the transition is:

```text
new_heading = old_heading + angle
```

For:

```text
WalkForward(distance)
```

the direction comes from:

```text
current_heading
```

This creates a deterministic spatial machine.

## 63. Spatial State Machine

```text
             TURN
              │
              ↓
        ┌───────────┐
        │  HEADING  │
        └─────┬─────┘
              │
          MOVE/FORWARD
              │
              ↓
        ┌───────────┐
        │  POSITION │
        └─────┬─────┘
              │
              ↓
       PATH DISTANCE
```

This is useful for navigation, games, robotics, and reasoning benchmarks.

## 64. Common Errors

### Error 1 — Confusing path and displacement

```text
3 km east + 4 km north
```

means:

```text
path = 7 km
displacement = 5 km
```

### Error 2 — Ignoring heading

```text
right
```

has no fixed absolute direction without a reference frame.

### Error 3 — Inventing distances

```text
A is north of B
```

does not mean:

```text
A is 1 km north of B
```

### Error 4 — Mixing coordinate conventions

The system must define which axis represents north.

### Error 5 — Losing units

```text
5 km + 3 m
```

must be normalized before arithmetic.

### Error 6 — Confusing heading and movement

An agent can face west while moving north.

### Error 7 — Treating a diagonal distance as two full components

“5 km north-east” is not automatically:

```text
(5,5)
```

if 5 km is the total diagonal distance.

## 65. Validation Rules

Useful rules include:

```text
RULE 1:
Distance cannot be negative.

RULE 2:
A movement must have a direction and magnitude unless one is intentionally unknown.

RULE 3:
Opposite movements must cancel correctly.

RULE 4:
Point - Point = displacement.

RULE 5:
Point + displacement = Point.

RULE 6:
Path distance >= straight-line displacement.

RULE 7:
Same start and end point implies zero displacement.

RULE 8:
Relative directions require a reference frame.

RULE 9:
Unknown distance remains unknown.

RULE 10:
All distance calculations use compatible units.

RULE 11:
Heading and movement direction are separate concepts.

RULE 12:
The coordinate convention must remain consistent.
```

## 66. Why This Is Better Than Pattern Matching

A model could memorize:

```text
5 km north + 3 km east → 5.83 km NE
```

but there are unlimited combinations.

A symbolic spatial engine learns reusable rules:

```text
direction → vector
movement → displacement
displacement → position
position difference → distance
coordinate signs → direction
```

Then it can solve new problems it has never memorized.

The important principle is:

> **Learn reusable spatial rules rather than memorizing every possible answer.**

## 67. Spatial Reasoning as Compilation

A powerful architecture is:

```text
Natural Language
       ↓
Lexer
       ↓
Parser
       ↓
Spatial AST
       ↓
Semantic Analysis
       ↓
Spatial IR
       ↓
Vector / Coordinate Engine
       ↓
Constraint Solver
       ↓
Geometry Engine
       ↓
Verifier
       ↓
Answer Generator
```

The sentence describes the problem.

The AST captures structure.

The spatial IR captures meaning.

The reasoning engine executes the spatial operations.

The verifier checks the result.
